@opposition_reset
# prose-audit: Grade A — KO, Poland 2050 and Lewica compete through social, democratic and local strategies with distinct organisational aftermaths.
title: The opposition resets
new-page: true
on-arrival: opposition_reset_event_done = 1; news_headline = "KO and Poland 2050 contest the same centre"

= June 2021 — Three opposition strategies

KO asks every opposition party to line up behind a single anti-PiS pole.
Szymon Hołownia presents Poland 2050 as an escape from the habits of the old
opposition and courts voters who want a new centre. Our organisers hear both
pitches as an invitation to become somebody else's left flank.

The choice is not whether to oppose PiS. It is where we intend to remain
distinct while doing it.

- @reset_social
- @reset_democratic
- @reset_local

@reset_social
title: Compete with both on wages, housing and services
on-arrival: opposition_reset_strategy = "Social competition"; labor_credibility += 5; workers_support += 3; public_sector_support += 2; ko_relation -= 3; p2050_relation -= 3; left_economic_position -= 2; news_headline = "Lewica opens a social flank against both centrist parties"

Our MPs take wages, rents and public services on tour while KO and Poland 2050
compete over who can best manage the centre. Both accuse us of dividing the
opposition. Trade unionists and tenants' groups hear something rarer: a reason
to support the Left that is not merely fear of PiS.

- @poland_events.dworczyk_hack_2021: Turn to the leaked mailbox


@reset_democratic
title: Rebuild the democratic-opposition table
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Requires one resource.
on-arrival: opposition_reset_strategy = "Democratic opposition table"; resources -= 1; ko_relation += 5; p2050_relation += 5; relation_ko_p2050 += 4; rival_relation_ko_p2050 += 4; constitutional_restraint += 3; razem_dissent += 3; news_headline = "The opposition table returns with written limits"

Lawyers and parliamentary whips sit down to agree on constitutional red lines,
procedural warnings and the votes on which nobody will surprise the others.
The table gives the opposition discipline without pretending it has one
programme. Razem still fears that every shared photograph teaches voters to
see us as a junior partner in somebody else's coalition.

- @poland_events.dworczyk_hack_2021: Turn to the leaked mailbox


@reset_local
title: Leave Warsaw to the leaders and organise counties
on-arrival: opposition_reset_strategy = "County organisation"; local_network += 5; rural_support += 3; psl_relation += 3; media_capacity -= 1; news_headline = "County organisation replaces another leaders' summit"

The next Warsaw summit takes place without us. Our money goes instead to county
offices, bus-stop meetings and organisers looking for credible local
candidates. It produces fewer evening headlines, but more people know whom to
call when a clinic closes. PSL notices that the Left has begun treating the
counties as political ground rather than scenery between cities.

- @poland_events.dworczyk_hack_2021: Turn to the leaked mailbox




@nik_banas_2019
# prose-audit: Grade B — Banaś, the office and the government that appointed him are all placed before the decision, and each route sets the chamber's independence for the rest of the game; the tenement's tenants are still described rather than heard.
title: The auditor PiS appointed
subtitle: Defend the audit chamber, demand its president's head, or trade the office for a campaign week
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
Q.nik_banas_2019_done = 1;
Q.institutional_issue_salience =
    (Number(Q.institutional_issue_salience) || 0) + 4;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Supreme Audit Office's new president is accused of hiding a fortune";
!}

= November 2019 — An institution nobody wanted to think about

<span class="tooltip-text" style="color:#666" title="Marian Banaś (b. 1955) — PiS finance minister and head of the tax administration, appointed president of the Supreme Audit Office in August 2019 for a six-year term the government cannot end at will."><b>Marian Banaś</b></span> was PiS's own choice for president of the <span class="tooltip-text" style="color:#666" title="Najwyższa Izba Kontroli (NIK) — the Supreme Audit Office. A constitutional body answerable to the Sejm, not the cabinet. It audits state spending, publishes binding findings and its president serves a fixed six-year term with immunity."><b>Najwyższa Izba Kontroli</b></span>, the Supreme Audit Office, three months ago: a loyal finance
minister, confirmed with the government's votes for a six-year term the
government cannot end at will. A television report now shows a Kraków tenement
he owns, income he did not declare, and tenants whose rent arrangements the
programme describes in language usually reserved for other kinds of business.

PiS wants him gone immediately, and discovers what it built. The NIK president
holds a fixed term, immunity, and an office whose whole purpose is to audit the
government. He is not resigning.

The Left has spent four years outside parliament arguing that institutions
matter more than the people occupying them. This is the first month in which
that sentence costs something.

[? if presidential_candidate = "Robert Biedroń": Our presidential campaign is
already built around a modern, European Poland, and an argument about audit
procedure is not the material Biedroń's people wanted.?]
[? if presidential_candidate = "Adrian Zandberg": Zandberg's campaign has been
looking for exactly this: an argument about who actually owns the state.?]
[? if presidential_candidate = "Undecided": With the nomination still open, no
campaign owns this argument yet, and none of our currents can be made to carry
it.?]
[? if independence_march_strategy = "Counter-mobilisation": After the march we
are already the party that spent November opposing rather than proposing.?]

- @nik19_institution
- @nik19_resignation
- @nik19_campaign
- @nik19_ignore

@nik19_institution
title: Defend the office and refuse to help the government retake it
subtitle: Locks the audit chamber's independence in for years; we will be accused of shielding a PiS appointee's property.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nik_stance = "Institutional defence";
Q.nik_independence = (Number(Q.nik_independence) || 50) + 22;
Q.nik_government_hostility =
    (Number(Q.nik_government_hostility) || 20) + 25;
Q.institutional_trust = (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) + 5;
Q.constitutional_restraint =
    (Number(Q.constitutional_restraint) || 0) + 5;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) - 2;
Q.pis_relation = (Number(Q.pis_relation) || 0) - 5;
Q.progressives_dissent =
    (Number(Q.progressives_dissent) || 0) + 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left defends the audit chamber's term against the party that filled it";
!}

Our line separates the man from the office in public, which is the least
popular sentence available in November. A fixed term exists precisely so that
an auditor cannot be removed by the people being audited, and we will not lend
votes to breaking it for a government that suddenly discovered the principle
of accountability.

The property allegations go to prosecutors, where they belong. The office keeps
its term. From this month the Supreme Audit Office has an interest in proving
its independence to somebody, and the only parliamentary party that defended it
is ours.

- @poland_events.marshal_2019: Turn to the Marshal of the Sejm

@nik19_resignation
title: Demand his resignation and a criminal referral
subtitle: The clean, popular answer; it hands the government the precedent that an inconvenient auditor can be forced out.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nik_stance = "Resignation demanded";
Q.nik_independence = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.nik_independence) || 50) - 12
);
Q.nik_government_hostility = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.nik_government_hostility) || 20) - 5
);
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 4;
Q.institutional_trust = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) - 3
);
Q.media_capacity = (Number(Q.media_capacity) || 0) + 2;
Q.pis_relation = (Number(Q.pis_relation) || 0) - 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "Lewica demands the audit chief resign over the undeclared tenement";
!}

We say what most of the country is saying: a man who cannot account for his own
property cannot audit anybody else's. The clip is good, the polling is
comfortable, and PiS quietly enjoys having the opposition make its argument for
it.

The precedent is the cost. An auditor with a constitutional term has been
declared removable by public pressure, and the government now has an opposition
quotation for every future occasion on which an inconvenient official needs to
go.

- @poland_events.marshal_2019: Turn to the Marshal of the Sejm

@nik19_campaign
title: Put the audit chamber at the centre of the presidential campaign
subtitle: Requires a settled nominee. Makes "who owns the state" the campaign's argument for the next six months.
choose-if: presidential_candidate != "Undecided"
unavailable-subtitle: The nomination is still open — no campaign exists yet to carry a national argument about the state.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nik_stance = "Campaign centrepiece";
Q.nik_independence = (Number(Q.nik_independence) || 50) + 12;
Q.nik_government_hostility =
    (Number(Q.nik_government_hostility) || 20) + 15;
Q.institutional_issue_salience =
    (Number(Q.institutional_issue_salience) || 0) + 6;
Q.media_capacity = (Number(Q.media_capacity) || 0) + 3;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 2;
Q.presidential_campaign_theme = "State capture and public accountability";
Q.institutional_trust = (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) + 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left's presidential campaign opens on state capture";
!}

**[+ presidential_candidate +]** takes the tenement, the appointment and the
attempted removal and turns them into one campaign sentence: they did not
capture the state to serve you, they captured it to serve themselves. It is the
first argument of ours in years that PiS cannot answer by changing the subject
to Brussels.

It also commits the campaign. Every subsequent month has to produce another
instalment of the same argument, and a campaign about institutions is a
campaign that has to keep finding institutions worth defending.

- @poland_events.marshal_2019: Turn to the Marshal of the Sejm

@nik19_ignore
title: Treat it as a quarrel inside the governing camp
subtitle: Costs nothing, decides nothing, and leaves the chamber's future to the people fighting over it.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nik_stance = "Stayed out";
Q.nik_independence = (Number(Q.nik_independence) || 50) - 4;
Q.nik_government_hostility =
    (Number(Q.nik_government_hostility) || 20) + 5;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) - 1;
Q.media_capacity = (Number(Q.media_capacity) || 0) - 1;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left watches PiS fight its own auditor";
!}

The caucus decides that a fight between a governing party and its own appointee
is not our fight, and spends the week on the presidential campaign instead.

PiS and its auditor tear at each other without our assistance. Whatever the
audit chamber becomes over the next six years, it will remember that the Left
was not in the room when its independence was decided.

- @poland_events.marshal_2019: Turn to the Marshal of the Sejm

@nik_removal_2020
# prose-audit: Grade B — The removal attempt reads back the November decision in prose and in what the player may still choose, and the audit-referral option is visibly locked to the earlier defence; the chamber's own auditors remain offstage.
title: The auditor they cannot remove
subtitle: PiS tries to end Banaś's term and finds the law it wrote
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
Q.nik_removal_2020_done = 1;
Q.nik_removal_pressure = 1;
Q.institutional_issue_salience =
    (Number(Q.institutional_issue_salience) || 0) + 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "The governing majority fails to remove the head of the audit chamber";
!}

= January 2020 — The term holds

Prosecutors move on the property file. The Sejm majority looks for a route to
end the term early and finds none that survives contact with the statute it
passed itself: dismissal requires grounds the government cannot manufacture in
a fortnight, and the immunity it granted is now working exactly as written.

Banaś stays. Within weeks the <span class="tooltip-text" style="color:#666" title="Najwyższa Izba Kontroli (NIK) — the Supreme Audit Office, a Sejm-answerable constitutional auditor whose president holds a fixed six-year term.">Supreme Audit Office</span> begins publishing findings
on government programmes with a bluntness nobody has seen from it in years — a
watchdog with nothing left to lose and a personal reason to bite.

[? if nik_stance = "Institutional defence": In November we defended the office
rather than the man, and the chamber's staff noticed which caucus did that.?]
[? if nik_stance = "Resignation demanded": In November we demanded his head.
The chamber's staff noticed that too, and the referral desk is not returning
our calls.?]
[? if nik_stance = "Campaign centrepiece": Our presidential campaign has been
running on state capture since November, and this month supplies the
evidence.?]
