= November 2020 — An appeal with a street attached

<span class="party-pis"><b>Jarosław Kaczyński</b></span>, deputy prime minister and leader of the governing
party, records an address calling on supporters to defend Polish churches from
the women's protests, in what he describes as a defence of the nation against
its destruction. Within days, groups of men in club colours and nationalist
organisers are standing outside churches in a dozen cities, some of them
photographing protesters, some of them not waiting to be photographed first.

The state has not deployed anybody. The leader of the largest governing party
has invited private citizens to police a protest, and the police are now
managing two crowds instead of one.

[? if abortion_strategy = "Autonomous movement support": Our October line was
to back the movement without directing it, so the organisers will decide what
protection they want and we will be asked to fund it.?]
[? if abortion_strategy = "Party-led mobilisation": We put our own name on the
October mobilisation, which means every confrontation outside a church this
month is reported with our banner in the frame.?]
[? if abortion_strategy = "Broad democratic front": Our October front runs
through KO and the centre, and half of it does not want to be photographed
arguing with churchgoers.?]
[? if abortion_strategy = "Palace restoration front": Our October route ran
through the Palace, and a president is a poor instrument against a crowd
somebody else called.?]
[? if womens_strike_followup = "Independent legal defence": The legal defence
network we funded after the first marches already has observers, lawyers and a
reporting form.?]

- @church20_police_duty
