Three caucuses agree a single position: no quoting from the channel, one joint
demand for a state information-security review, and a written rule for what
each party does the next time a mailbox appears. It is the first thing this
opposition has agreed without a summit photograph.

The standard is small and it holds. When surveillance and hacking return to
Polish politics — and they return within six months — there is already a
document with three signatures on it, and none of the signatories has to invent
a position under pressure.

