@marshal_2019
# prose-audit: Grade B — Kuchciński's flights, his resignation and Witek's election are placed as one chain, and the misuse-of-office register is locked to the November audit-chamber decision; the pilots and crews who flew the routes remain a category.
title: The Marshal's aircraft
subtitle: The chair changed hands in August over government flights; the new chamber confirms it and the rules stay unwritten
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
Q.marshal_2019_done = 1;
Q.office_misuse_salience =
    (Number(Q.office_misuse_salience) || 0) + 20;
Q.institutional_issue_salience =
    (Number(Q.institutional_issue_salience) || 0) + 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "The new Sejm confirms the Marshal installed after her predecessor's flight scandal";
!}

= November 2019 — The office and the aircraft

The last Sejm's Marshal, <span class="party-pis"><b>Marek Kuchciński</b></span>, resigned in August after
journalists counted his flights on government aircraft: about a hundred of
them, a large number to his own constituency, several carrying his family.
There was no criminal charge. There was an itemised list, published, of a
public office being used as a car service, and a governing party that
discovered it could not defend the list for more than a fortnight.

<span class="party-pis"><b>Elżbieta Witek</b></span> took the chair the same day he left it and has held it
since. At the first sitting of the new chamber the majority simply confirms
her, which is a formality: PiS has the votes, the nomination and no reason to
reopen a question it closed in August.

So the chair is not in play and we should be honest with ourselves about that.
Forty-nine deputies out of four hundred and sixty cannot elect a Marshal and
will not. What is in play is everything the office does afterwards — the
calendar, the order paper, which divisions are re-run, and an expenses regime
that August proved is legible to anybody who can read a flight log.

This is the one sitting of the term at which the majority is embarrassed about
that office. It is the cheapest day in four years to put a rule on the record.

[? if nik_stance = "Institutional defence": Having spent this month defending
the audit chamber's independence, we have the one institution in the country
that can put flight logs, cars and residences into a statutory finding.?]
[? if nik_stance = "Resignation demanded": We spent this month demanding an
auditor's resignation over undeclared property, which makes a demand about a
Marshal's flights consistent and slightly repetitive.?]
[? if presidential_candidate != "Undecided": **[+ presidential_candidate +]** is
about to spend six months being asked what the Left would do differently with
public office, and this is the cheapest available answer.?]

- @marshal19_register
- @marshal19_rules
- @marshal19_candidate
- @marshal19_pass

